---
epr-meta-version: 1
id: claude-hooks-governance
covers: subtree
rules:
  - id: per-turn-injector-carries-its-budget
    class: inject
    when:
      write: "*.py"
      contains-any: ["UserPromptSubmit"]
    dedupe-of: ".claude/hooks/run-projection.py (the worked example — cache-keyed derivation, event-correct emission, module-level RETIRE_WHEN, measured timeout)"
    why: >
      A hook on the PROMPT path is the most expensive intervenor shape in this tree: its cost is
      paid on EVERY turn, so a 2s derivation is a 2s tax on every exchange for the life of the
      session, and a hook that blocks a turn is worse than one that says nothing. Four properties
      make a per-turn injector admissible, and each has already been got wrong somewhere in this
      tree or its siblings. (1) THE HOT PATH READS A CACHE — keyed on cheap stats, never a
      subprocess chain; refresh the expensive leg on SessionStart and degrade the per-turn path to
      an honest `refresh pending` marker rather than blocking. (2) THE EMISSION SHAPE IS
      EVENT-SPECIFIC — on UserPromptSubmit plain stdout reaches model context, on
      SessionStart/PreToolUse only the `hookSpecificOutput` JSON wrapper lands; the wrong shape
      ships a hook that silently never lands (recorded at pickup-semantic-surfacing.py:182-188).
      (3) THE TIMEOUT IS MEASURED, NOT WISHED — values are SECONDS, and this file already carries
      a live declared-vs-actual mismatch (load-project-context.py registers 5s while its own
      internal budget is 25s, unreachable by construction). Measure the cached path, then declare
      a number the work fits inside; if it does not fit, shrink the derivation. (4) IT DECLARES
      `RETIRE_WHEN` — a per-turn scaffold is exactly the class that is load-bearing on one model
      tier and dead weight on the next, so the exit is part of shipping it, not a footnote.
    retire-when: >
      when the harness carries durable per-turn run-state injection natively — evidenced by zero
      `UserPromptSubmit` entries left in .claude/settings.json across two consecutive model-tier
      landings — at which point there is no per-turn injector in this tree for the advisory to
      instruct
  - id: correction-outlives-the-turn
    class: inject
    when:
      write: "*.py"
      contains-any: ["CORRECTION", "supersede", "no longer", "was wrong", "reverting"]
    dedupe-of: ".eprfs/status/flows.jsonl via `epr flow note --on <target> --kind correction --reason '...'` (the durable home for a run-scale correction)"
    why: >
      This edit reads as a CORRECTION — it reverses or supersedes an earlier decision. The reason
      for a reversal is the most perishable state in a run: it lands in the conversation, and
      compaction is lossy summarization of exactly that region, so nothing re-injects it afterward
      because habits and commitments were never told. Survival then depends on the summarizer's
      judgment, which is an outdated judgment left in charge. Worse, an operator's mid-run
      correction IS the algedonic channel: if the pain signal reaches only the current draft and
      not the state, it was absorbed by the thing that caused it. Write the correction where it
      outlives the window — `epr flow note --on <target> --kind correction --reason "…"` (or
      `--kind failed-approach --reason "tried X, it failed because Y" --switched-to "Z"`, so the
      next session inherits the consequence without re-attempting the dead end). The note
      ANNOTATES: it never discharges the commitment. This is a teaching signal, not a gate — it
      fires beside the per-turn projection's footer, which teaches the same write path at read
      time; the rule is the edit-time half of one discipline.
    retire-when: >
      when the write path is habitual rather than prompted — measured as four consecutive weeks in
      which every `run:correction` / `run:failed-approach` note in .eprfs/status/flows.jsonl was
      authored in a session where this rule did not fire first (the nudge is describing a habit,
      not producing one)
  - id: bootstrapping-head-is-recall-open-run-projection
    class: inject
    when:
      # EXACT filename, not a glob. `*project*.py` (this rule's first cut, 2026-09-11) matched
      # `memory-index-projection.py` too — that hook has nothing to do with the bootstrapping
      # head, and `fnmatch` has no alternation operator (the task brief's literal `a|b` pattern
      # never matches either target filename under this file's grammar; a broader glob is not a
      # safe substitute either, as this exact false-positive proved). Two rules, one per exact
      # filename, sharing the same `why`/`dedupe-of` text, is the correct shape for an "exactly
      # these two files" concern under a matcher with no alternation and no list support for
      # `when.write` (`_matches_when` in `_lib/epr_meta.py` calls `pat.lower()` on the value,
      # which requires a plain string).
      write: "run-projection.py"
    dedupe-of: "epr flow memory recall open --purpose bootstrap (the bootstrapping head; rendered by .claude/hooks/load-project-context.py at `minimal` and .claude/hooks/run-projection.py at `simple`)"
    why: >
      the bootstrapping head is `epr flow memory recall open --purpose bootstrap`; a hook renders
      it at a lens and never derives a second orientation. Station two of the governed-discovery
      plan (2026-09-11) retired both hooks' bespoke habits.yaml / flows.jsonl derivations (the
      former `get_habits_status` re-scan, and `run-projection.py`'s whole habits/saga/stocks
      derivation plus its private cache) — the SessionStart headline and the per-turn run-plane
      line are now thin renderers of ONE native recall session, at `minimal` and `simple`
      respectively. Re-deriving a top-red, WIP-fence, saga or commitments-stock reading inside
      either hook body — even a small one, even "just this one field" — is exactly the
      second-orientation drift this rule exists to catch before it regrows the cache/derivation
      pair station two just removed.
    retire-when: >
      when these two hook bodies are deleted or merged into a single thin transport (the per-hook
      distinction this rule polices no longer exists to drift), or when the harness itself
      resolves the bootstrapping head natively per turn and neither file shells to `epr flow
      memory recall open` at all — at which point there is no hook body left for the rule to
      keep honest
  - id: bootstrapping-head-is-recall-open-load-project-context
    class: inject
    when:
      write: "load-project-context.py"
    dedupe-of: "epr flow memory recall open --purpose bootstrap (the bootstrapping head; rendered by .claude/hooks/load-project-context.py at `minimal` and .claude/hooks/run-projection.py at `simple`)"
    why: >
      the bootstrapping head is `epr flow memory recall open --purpose bootstrap`; a hook renders
      it at a lens and never derives a second orientation. Station two of the governed-discovery
      plan (2026-09-11) retired both hooks' bespoke habits.yaml / flows.jsonl derivations (the
      former `get_habits_status` re-scan, and `run-projection.py`'s whole habits/saga/stocks
      derivation plus its private cache) — the SessionStart headline and the per-turn run-plane
      line are now thin renderers of ONE native recall session, at `minimal` and `simple`
      respectively. Re-deriving a top-red, WIP-fence, saga or commitments-stock reading inside
      either hook body — even a small one, even "just this one field" — is exactly the
      second-orientation drift this rule exists to catch before it regrows the cache/derivation
      pair station two just removed.
    retire-when: >
      when these two hook bodies are deleted or merged into a single thin transport (the per-hook
      distinction this rule polices no longer exists to drift), or when the harness itself
      resolves the bootstrapping head natively per turn and neither file shells to `epr flow
      memory recall open` at all — at which point there is no hook body left for the rule to
      keep honest
  - id: capability-tier-gate-owns-destructive-git
    class: inject
    when:
      write: "*.py"
      contains-any:
        - "git reset --hard"
        - "git push --force"
        - "git branch -D"
        - "git worktree remove --force"
        - "destructive-git-requires-tier"
    dedupe-of: ".claude/epr-meta/policies.yaml row `destructive-git-requires-tier@1` (the declared pattern / tier-order / tier-floor / remedy table; read directly by .claude/hooks/capability-tier-gate.py, the ONE PreToolUse enforcer)"
    why: >
      this edit is writing destructive-git command text into a `.py` file in this tree — exactly
      the shape of a second, hand-authored copy of the pattern table
      `capability-tier-gate.py` already reads from `destructive-git-requires-tier@1`. Task 2.4
      (2026-09-11, after a Haiku subagent's `git reset --hard` wiped another lane's uncommitted
      work on the shared `dev` checkout) put the table in exactly one place on purpose: a second
      copy — in a new hook, a helper, a test fixture standing in for the real table — can drift
      from the registry silently, and a drifted copy that under-lists patterns is a silent
      widening of what any tier may run. If this edit is the registry row itself or the gate
      hook's own tests, it is fine as written; if it is a NEW enforcer or a helper re-deriving
      the pattern/tier list, bind `.claude/epr-meta/policies.yaml`'s
      `destructive-git-requires-tier@1` instead of re-typing the patterns.
    retire-when: >
      when there is exactly one enforcer of the destructive-git table for four consecutive
      months with no second hand-authored copy found by this rule or by a drift audit — the
      nudge is describing a habit, not producing one
---

`.claude/hooks/` — the thin hook-transport layer: PreToolUse gates (epr-meta compose-gate,
managed-surface discipline, sensitive-file/seed/cargo guards) and PostToolUse signal
accumulators (drift counters, sentinels). This manifest CLAIMS full responsibility for the
subtree (`covers: subtree`): the coverage walk terminates here.

**Still structural, and still not enforceable as a predicate.** The real gates on this tree
are: (1) a hook file is INERT until registered in `.claude/settings.json` — an unregistered
hook is dead code, and registration is the review surface; (2) hooks are fail-open by contract
(any internal error exits 0 silently) with per-entry `timeout` in SECONDS (normalized
2026-07-02 — values had been written as milliseconds, making the declared 2-15s budgets
33min-4h no-ops); (3) hooks stay THIN — real logic lives in `.claude/scripts/_lib/` (tested
under `_lib/__tests__/`) or `.epr-meta/elohim/lenses/` (tested under its own `__tests__/`),
so the transport can be swapped (the brit/eprfs direction) without losing the rules. None of
those three is expressible as an enforced `.epr-meta` predicate, and none has been made one.

**Five `class: inject` teaching signals** under this manifest's own admission bar ("only if a
NEW recurring, mechanizable drift appears in this tree"), all advisory and all carrying their
exit. Two, added 2026-08-13: `per-turn-injector-carries-its-budget` fires when a hook file
names `UserPromptSubmit`: the per-turn path is a genuinely new shape here — before the
run-plane projection landed, this tree had exactly ONE prompt-path hook — and its four
admissibility properties (cached hot path, event-correct emission shape, measured timeout,
declared `RETIRE_WHEN`) are each recorded in this tree as an existing miss, not a hypothetical.
`correction-outlives-the-turn` fires on a correction-shaped edit and points at `epr flow note`
— the edit-time half of the discipline whose read-time half is the projection block's footer.
Two more, added 2026-09-11 (station two of the governed-discovery plan):
`bootstrapping-head-is-recall-open-run-projection` and
`bootstrapping-head-is-recall-open-load-project-context` fire on an edit to their EXACT
namesake filename — split into two rules, one per exact filename, rather than one glob, after
a first cut (`write: "*project*.py"`) was found to also match `memory-index-projection.py`
(fnmatch has no alternation, and `"project"` is a substring of `"projection"`) — and point at
`epr flow memory recall open --purpose bootstrap` as the one place either hook's orientation
comes from. One more, added 2026-09-11 (Task 2.4, after a Haiku subagent's `git reset --hard`
wiped another lane's uncommitted work on the shared `dev` checkout):
`capability-tier-gate-owns-destructive-git` fires when destructive-git command text appears in
a `.py` file, pointing at the one declared pattern table
(`.claude/epr-meta/policies.yaml`'s `destructive-git-requires-tier@1`) rather than a second,
driftable copy. None of the five blocks; each retires on a counted condition.
